<p>I was at <a href="https://www.caseyliss.com/">Casey Liss</a>&#8216;s house over the weekend, sitting in his living room, when suddenly I heard a chime and a voice said, out of nowhere: &#8220;The wash is done.&#8221;</p>
<p>It turns out that Casey has <a href="https://www.caseyliss.com/2025/8/25/mailbox-delivery-notifications">connected an array of sensors</a> to his <a href="https://www.home-assistant.io/">Home Assistant</a> setup, and somehow his family accepts that occasionally a disembodied voice will shout out home status information. (It&#8217;s not as subtle as <a href="https://www.caseyliss.com/2026/2/2/status-board">his other approach</a>.)</p>
<p>What&#8217;s wilder is that the box Casey&#8217;s using to do this is actually one I have in my own home! It&#8217;s a YoLink Speaker Hub, which I bought <a href="https://link.amazon/B0glmLdux">along with three leak sensors</a> (affiliate link; I bought a three-pack of sensors with the hub for $60) a year and a half ago. And while I haven&#8217;t automated my speaker hub to announce things (yet!), I realized I haven&#8217;t sufficiently sung the praises of these YoLink sensors.</p>
<p>In short: They are very good, with tremendous range and battery life, and extremely easy to install. You don&#8217;t actually need anything like Home Assistant to use them—they come with their own, entirely functional app for configuration and alert purposes.</p>
<p>The leak sensors themselves are tiny plastic pucks you just put in potential trouble spots—for me, that&#8217;s under the kitchen sink, next to the washing machine, and next to the condensate pump that doubles as the outlet for my dehumidifier in the winter months. When they register a leak, I get an emergency notification on my phone and (optionally) the Speaker Hub can make an unmissable alert noise.</p>
<p>We&#8217;ve previously had under-sink and washing-machine disasters, so this was an easy purchase for me. And since I installed them, I&#8217;ve been alerted once about a serious issue—the drain hose from my dehumidifier had come out of the pump housing and was dripping freely.</p>
<p>Casey&#8217;s also taking advantage of the long battery life and range of YoLink&#8217;s system to do things like use contact sensors to alert him when his mail has been delivered, and the company offers a <a href="https://shop.yosmart.com/collections/all">wild array of sensors</a> that work with the hub.</p>
<p>And, yes, once you plug the Speaker Hub into Home Assistant, you can drive its built-in audio features, which include built-in text-to-speech, a library of alert tones, and even custom MP3 sounds, from any automation happening in your house. (Casey&#8217;s laundry alerts are, impossibly, <em>not</em> from smart appliances, but from inline energy-consumption monitors he&#8217;s attached to his washer and dryer.)</p>
<p>If you&#8217;re so inclined, you can really go to town with these sensors. But if you just want basic operation, such as making sure that the next time your sink leaks it&#8217;s not a total disaster, I can&#8217;t recommend the YoLink sensors and hub highly enough.</p>
